Test images for .github/probes/pe-alignment-status.ps1
=====================================================

Four prebuilt PE32 executables used to ask one question of a real NT kernel:
does it load a PE whose SectionAlignment is smaller than the page size?

They are committed as binaries deliberately.  They are NOT generated at probe
time: a generated image would measure this repo's generator, and the point of
the probe is to measure NT.

Source
------
hi.c (committed alongside, 51 bytes):

    #include <windows.h>
    int main(void) { return 42; }

sha256 of hi.c: 2f61c6ada042773cdd7ac63a41a3c3c5cdbba5346300c9b08bb0d1638fd7c0da

Producing toolchain
-------------------
Reported by the author of the images: tcc (the Tiny C Compiler), targeting
win32/i386.

This is REPORTED, not measured.  The binaries carry no embedded toolchain
string (searched: no "tcc" substring anywhere in any of the four files), and
they could not be reproduced on the machine that assembled this directory --
its tcc is 0.9.27 (x86_64 Linux) with no win32 headers or crt, so it cannot
emit a PE at all.  The exact tcc version and the exact link flags that set the
sub-page alignments are therefore NOT established here.  The sha256 sums below
are the identity of these files; the toolchain attribution is hearsay.

What IS measured from the bytes (by python3 struct, on the files as copied):

    file             size   Machine  Magic   SectAlign  FileAlign  SizeOfImage  Subsystem
    default.exe      2048   0x014c   0x010b  0x1000     0x200      0x4000       3
    subpage20.exe    1568   0x014c   0x010b  0x0020     0x0020     0x0460       3
    subpage200.exe   2048   0x014c   0x010b  0x0200     0x0200     0x0a00       3
    native20.exe     1568   0x014c   0x010b  0x0020     0x0020     0x0460       1

    All four: e_lfanew=0x80, "PE\0\0", 3 sections (.text/.rdata/.bss),
    SizeOfOptionalHeader=0xe0, LinkerVersion 6.0, TimeDateStamp=0,
    imports from kernel32.dll and msvcrt.dll.

    Machine 0x014c is i386 and Magic 0x010b is PE32.  All four cells share
    that, so it is constant along the alignment axis -- but note that on an
    x64 CI runner every CreateProcessW here goes through WOW64.

sha256
------
489368b986ca21ae98486a2a7989a5e1fc756a17c6b80fb34b90401d5122b863  subpage20.exe
75c10877d50bba918d3751701ef07457b789e1743433807aaf9eb18c438a9b5a  subpage200.exe
a3f7b535e1bfc753d920478035eddc130c25fc1361321d5b6722a8e528cf1b2c  native20.exe
b3b4817f4b8bf0688af96a2652f026ad71c19ed8d2ac82df38263f710d67ad0f  default.exe

The probe recomputes these at run time on the runner and prints measured
against expected, so a mangled checkout shows up as a hash mismatch rather
than as a mysterious NTSTATUS.  .gitattributes here marks *.exe binary so no
eol conversion can touch them.

Roles
-----
default.exe      positive control.  Ordinary 0x1000/0x200 alignment.  If this
                 does not map as SEC_IMAGE and launch, the harness is broken
                 and the whole run is void.
subpage200.exe   SectionAlignment == FileAlignment == 0x200.  This is the cell
                 the probe exists for.
subpage20.exe    SectionAlignment == FileAlignment == 0x20.
native20.exe     Subsystem 1 (native).  Only meaningful on the
                 NtCreateSection(SEC_IMAGE) axis; its CreateProcessW result
                 says nothing about alignment.
